===============================================================================
The exception: what toopo copies into your project is MIT-0
===============================================================================

Some files in this repository are licensed under the MIT No Attribution licence
(MIT-0) instead of the MIT licence above. Each one says so in its own first two
lines:

    // typescript/array/group-by@1 - https://toopo.dev/typescript/array/group-by@1/
    // SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT-0

A file carrying that SPDX identifier is under MIT-0, whose full text is at the
end of this file. Every other file in this repository is under the MIT licence
above.


Which files, and why it is not a list
-------------------------------------

They are exactly the files `toopo add` copies into your project. Today that is
one implementation file per contract, and the repository derives the set from
the installer's own answer rather than from a list somebody wrote down: a
hand-written list of paths goes stale the day a contract gains a second file,
and the failure is silent and lands in somebody else's repository. A test
resolves the marked files against what the installer would copy, in both
directions - nothing copied is unmarked, and nothing unmarked is copied.


Why MIT-0 and not MIT
---------------------

Toopo does not ship you a dependency. It copies source code into your
repository, and that code becomes yours to read, edit and maintain. The MIT
licence would make that copy carry an obligation: its clause above requires the
copyright notice *and the permission notice* - the whole paragraph - to travel
with every substantial portion of the software.

Two things follow, and both are bad. Satisfying it literally means pasting
about 1 100 characters of licence text at the top of a 2 kB function, which is
a third of what you receive and a third of what your reviewers read. Satisfying
it with a one-line identifier is the ordinary convention and is not what the
clause asks for, so every user would be quietly non-compliant for a function
they now maintain themselves.

MIT-0 is the MIT licence with that clause removed. It is OSI-approved and
recognised by the usual licence scanners. Under it you owe nothing: no notice,
no attribution, no mention anywhere. You may delete the two header lines the
moment the file lands.

We would rather you kept them, and the reason is yours rather than ours: the
first line is the address of the contract that file was verified against, so
whoever finds it in six months can look up what it is meant to do and check it
still does. That is provenance, and it is a service, not a condition.


